WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.230
XG boost, it's extreme

00:00:04.240 --> 00:00:06.110
and it's gradient boost.

00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:09.830
StatQuest

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:12.430
Hello, I'm Josh Starmer and welcome to

00:00:12.440 --> 00:00:14.590
StatQuest. Today we're going to talk

00:00:14.600 --> 00:00:18.030
about XG boost part one. We're going to

00:00:18.040 --> 00:00:20.390
talk about XG boost trees and how

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:23.430
they're used for regression.

00:00:23.440 --> 00:00:26.070
Note, this StatQuest assumes that you

00:00:26.080 --> 00:00:27.950
are already familiar with at least the

00:00:27.960 --> 00:00:30.910
main ideas of how gradient boost does

00:00:30.920 --> 00:00:32.870
regression.

00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:35.030
And you should be familiar with at least

00:00:35.040 --> 00:00:37.990
the main ideas behind regularization.

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:40.790
If not, check out the quests. The links

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:44.030
are in the description below.

00:00:44.040 --> 00:00:47.590
XG boost is extreme

00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:49.710
and that means it's a big machine

00:00:49.720 --> 00:00:52.870
learning algorithm with lots of parts.

00:00:52.880 --> 00:00:54.790
The good news is that each part is

00:00:54.800 --> 00:00:57.470
pretty simple and easy to understand and

00:00:57.480 --> 00:00:59.270
we'll go through them one step at a

00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:01.310
time.

00:01:01.320 --> 00:01:03.830
Actually, I'm assuming that you are

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:06.270
already familiar with gradient boost and

00:01:06.280 --> 00:01:08.710
regularization.

00:01:08.720 --> 00:01:11.110
So we'll start by learning about XG

00:01:11.120 --> 00:01:14.870
boost's unique regression trees.

00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:17.590
Because this is a big topic, we'll spend

00:01:17.600 --> 00:01:20.990
three whole StatQuests on it.

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:24.390
In this StatQuest, part one, we'll build

00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:27.070
our intuition about how XG boost does

00:01:27.080 --> 00:01:31.190
regression with its unique trees.

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:34.150
In part two, we'll build our intuition

00:01:34.160 --> 00:01:39.070
about how XG boost does classification.

00:01:39.080 --> 00:01:41.550
And in part three, we'll dive into the

00:01:41.560 --> 00:01:43.910
mathematical details and show you how

00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:46.190
regression and classification are

00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:48.910
related and why creating unique trees

00:01:48.920 --> 00:01:51.710
makes so much sense.

00:01:51.720 --> 00:01:54.910
Note, XG boost was designed to be used

00:01:54.920 --> 00:01:58.990
with large, complicated data sets.

00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:01.190
However, to keep the examples from

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:03.390
getting out of hand, we'll use this

00:02:03.400 --> 00:02:06.670
super simple training data.

00:02:06.680 --> 00:02:09.190
On the x-axis, we have different drug

00:02:09.200 --> 00:02:11.550
dosages.

00:02:11.560 --> 00:02:14.030
And on the y-axis, we've measured drug

00:02:14.040 --> 00:02:16.670
effectiveness.

00:02:16.680 --> 00:02:18.870
These two observations have relatively

00:02:18.880 --> 00:02:20.870
large positive values for drug

00:02:20.880 --> 00:02:22.990
effectiveness, and that means that the

00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:25.630
drug was helpful.

00:02:25.640 --> 00:02:27.910
These two observations have relatively

00:02:27.920 --> 00:02:29.949
large negative values for drug

00:02:29.959 --> 00:02:32.070
effectiveness, and that means that the

00:02:32.080 --> 00:02:35.949
drug did more harm than good.

00:02:35.959 --> 00:02:38.830
The very first step in fitting XGBoost

00:02:38.840 --> 00:02:40.830
to the training data is to make an

00:02:40.840 --> 00:02:43.070
initial prediction.

00:02:43.080 --> 00:02:45.590
This prediction can be anything, but by

00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:48.430
default, it is 0.5,

00:02:48.440 --> 00:02:50.110
regardless of whether you're using

00:02:50.120 --> 00:02:52.230
XGBoost for regression or

00:02:52.240 --> 00:02:54.910
classification.

00:02:54.920 --> 00:02:57.630
The prediction, 0.5,

00:02:57.640 --> 00:03:00.030
corresponds to this thick, black,

00:03:00.040 --> 00:03:02.750
horizontal line.

00:03:02.760 --> 00:03:04.790
And the residuals, the differences

00:03:04.800 --> 00:03:06.710
between the observed and predicted

00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:09.190
values, show us how good the initial

00:03:09.200 --> 00:03:11.870
prediction is.

00:03:11.880 --> 00:03:15.710
Now, just like unextreme gradient boost,

00:03:15.720 --> 00:03:18.150
XGBoost fits a regression tree to the

00:03:18.160 --> 00:03:20.070
residuals.

00:03:20.080 --> 00:03:23.070
However, unlike unextreme gradient

00:03:23.080 --> 00:03:25.710
boost, which typically uses regular,

00:03:25.720 --> 00:03:28.550
off-the-shelf regression trees,

00:03:28.560 --> 00:03:31.830
XGBoost uses a unique regression tree

00:03:31.840 --> 00:03:35.750
that I call an XGBoost tree.

00:03:35.760 --> 00:03:37.509
So, let's talk about how to build an

00:03:37.519 --> 00:03:41.190
XGBoost tree for regression.

00:03:41.200 --> 00:03:43.630
Note, there are many ways to build

00:03:43.640 --> 00:03:47.270
XGBoost trees. This video focuses on the

00:03:47.280 --> 00:03:49.190
most common way to build them for

00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:51.870
regression.

00:03:51.880 --> 00:03:56.230
Each tree starts out as a single leaf.

00:03:56.240 --> 00:04:00.590
And all of the residuals go to the leaf.

00:04:00.600 --> 00:04:03.590
Now we calculate a quality score or

00:04:03.600 --> 00:04:07.870
similarity score for the residuals.

00:04:07.880 --> 00:04:11.150
The similarity score equals the sum of

00:04:11.160 --> 00:04:14.630
the residuals squared over the number of

00:04:14.640 --> 00:04:18.390
residuals plus lambda.

00:04:18.400 --> 00:04:21.270
Note, lambda is a regularization

00:04:21.280 --> 00:04:23.630
parameter and we'll talk more about that

00:04:23.640 --> 00:04:25.310
later.

00:04:25.320 --> 00:04:29.750
For now, let lambda equals zero.

00:04:29.760 --> 00:04:32.230
Now we plug the four residuals into the

00:04:32.240 --> 00:04:34.510
numerator.

00:04:34.520 --> 00:04:36.430
And since there are four residuals in

00:04:36.440 --> 00:04:38.870
the leaf, we put a four in the

00:04:38.880 --> 00:04:41.110
denominator.

00:04:41.120 --> 00:04:43.430
Note, because we do not square the

00:04:43.440 --> 00:04:45.909
residuals before we add them together in

00:04:45.919 --> 00:04:50.710
the numerator, 7.5 and -7.5

00:04:50.720 --> 00:04:53.430
cancel each other out.

00:04:53.440 --> 00:04:55.430
In other words, when we add this

00:04:55.440 --> 00:04:56.790
residual

00:04:56.800 --> 00:04:58.990
to this residual,

00:04:59.000 --> 00:05:02.070
they cancel each other out.

00:05:02.080 --> 00:05:08.390
Likewise, 6.5 cancels out most of -10.5,

00:05:08.400 --> 00:05:13.030
leaving -4 squared in the numerator.

00:05:13.040 --> 00:05:15.350
Thus, the similarity score for the

00:05:15.360 --> 00:05:19.190
residuals in the root equals four.

00:05:19.200 --> 00:05:22.190
So let's put similarity equals four up

00:05:22.200 --> 00:05:25.630
here so we can keep track of it.

00:05:25.640 --> 00:05:27.990
Now the question is whether or not we

00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:30.430
can do a better job clustering similar

00:05:30.440 --> 00:05:32.870
residuals if we split them into two

00:05:32.880 --> 00:05:34.870
groups.

00:05:34.880 --> 00:05:37.430
To answer this, we first focus on the

00:05:37.440 --> 00:05:39.550
two observations with the lowest

00:05:39.560 --> 00:05:41.990
dosages.

00:05:42.000 --> 00:05:44.909
Their average dosage is 15 and that

00:05:44.919 --> 00:05:49.350
corresponds to this dotted red line.

00:05:49.360 --> 00:05:51.670
So, we split the observations into two

00:05:51.680 --> 00:05:53.750
groups based on whether or not the

00:05:53.760 --> 00:05:57.390
dosage is less than 15.

00:05:57.400 --> 00:05:59.870
The observation on the far left is the

00:05:59.880 --> 00:06:03.230
only one with dosage less than 15.

00:06:03.240 --> 00:06:05.670
So, its residual goes to the leaf on the

00:06:05.680 --> 00:06:07.630
left.

00:06:07.640 --> 00:06:09.710
All of the other residuals go to the

00:06:09.720 --> 00:06:12.830
leaf on the right.

00:06:12.840 --> 00:06:15.030
Now, we calculate the similarity score

00:06:15.040 --> 00:06:17.909
for the leaf on the left by plugging the

00:06:17.919 --> 00:06:21.510
one residual into the numerator.

00:06:21.520 --> 00:06:23.670
And since only one residual went to the

00:06:23.680 --> 00:06:25.550
leaf on the left, the number of

00:06:25.560 --> 00:06:28.990
residuals equals one.

00:06:29.000 --> 00:06:31.550
And just like before, we set lambda

00:06:31.560 --> 00:06:34.070
equal to zero.

00:06:34.080 --> 00:06:36.190
And the similarity score for the leaf on

00:06:36.200 --> 00:06:40.990
the left equals 110.25.

00:06:41.000 --> 00:06:45.630
So, let's put similarity equals 110.25

00:06:45.640 --> 00:06:47.710
under the leaf so we can keep track of

00:06:47.720 --> 00:06:49.430
it.

00:06:49.440 --> 00:06:51.630
And calculate the similarity score for

00:06:51.640 --> 00:06:53.790
the residuals that go to the leaf on the

00:06:53.800 --> 00:06:55.790
right.

00:06:55.800 --> 00:06:58.550
We plug in the sum of residuals squared

00:06:58.560 --> 00:07:01.190
into the numerator.

00:07:01.200 --> 00:07:03.030
And since there are three residuals in

00:07:03.040 --> 00:07:05.630
the leaf on the right, we plug three

00:07:05.640 --> 00:07:08.390
into the denominator.

00:07:08.400 --> 00:07:11.190
And just like before, let's let lambda

00:07:11.200 --> 00:07:13.750
equal zero.

00:07:13.760 --> 00:07:16.990
Note, like we saw earlier, because we do

00:07:17.000 --> 00:07:19.390
not square the residuals before we add

00:07:19.400 --> 00:07:23.990
them together, 7.5 and -7.5

00:07:24.000 --> 00:07:26.830
cancel each other out.

00:07:26.840 --> 00:07:30.110
Leaving only one residual, 6.5, in the

00:07:30.120 --> 00:07:32.750
numerator.

00:07:32.760 --> 00:07:35.070
Thus, the similarity score for the

00:07:35.080 --> 00:07:37.510
residuals in the leaf on the right

00:07:37.520 --> 00:07:41.550
equals 14.08.

00:07:41.560 --> 00:07:45.630
So, let's similarity equals 14.08

00:07:45.640 --> 00:07:47.590
under the leaf so we can keep track of

00:07:47.600 --> 00:07:49.670
it.

00:07:49.680 --> 00:07:51.870
Now that we have calculated similarity

00:07:51.880 --> 00:07:54.590
scores for each node,

00:07:54.600 --> 00:07:56.870
we see that when the residuals in a node

00:07:56.880 --> 00:07:59.110
are very different, they cancel each

00:07:59.120 --> 00:08:01.390
other out and the similarity score is

00:08:01.400 --> 00:08:04.110
relatively small.

00:08:04.120 --> 00:08:06.470
In contrast, when the residuals are

00:08:06.480 --> 00:08:09.350
similar or there is just one of them,

00:08:09.360 --> 00:08:11.150
they do not cancel out and the

00:08:11.160 --> 00:08:15.830
similarity score is relatively large.

00:08:15.840 --> 00:08:18.150
Now we need to quantify how much better

00:08:18.160 --> 00:08:20.710
the leaves cluster similar residuals

00:08:20.720 --> 00:08:23.350
than the root.

00:08:23.360 --> 00:08:25.909
We do this by calculating the gain of

00:08:25.919 --> 00:08:29.909
splitting the residuals into two groups.

00:08:29.919 --> 00:08:32.510
Gain is equal to the similarity score

00:08:32.520 --> 00:08:34.990
for the leaf on the left

00:08:35.000 --> 00:08:37.230
plus the similarity score for the leaf

00:08:37.240 --> 00:08:38.990
on the right

00:08:39.000 --> 00:08:43.190
minus the similarity score for the root.

00:08:43.200 --> 00:08:49.070
Pluging in the numbers

00:08:49.080 --> 00:08:52.750
gives us 120.33.

00:08:52.760 --> 00:08:55.470
Small bam.

00:08:55.480 --> 00:08:57.670
Now that we have calculated the gain for

00:08:57.680 --> 00:09:01.150
the threshold of dosage less than 15, we

00:09:01.160 --> 00:09:03.390
can compare it to the gain calculated

00:09:03.400 --> 00:09:06.070
for other thresholds.

00:09:06.080 --> 00:09:08.710
So, we shift the threshold over so that

00:09:08.720 --> 00:09:10.950
it is the average of the next two

00:09:10.960 --> 00:09:13.150
observations

00:09:13.160 --> 00:09:15.590
and build a simple tree that divides the

00:09:15.600 --> 00:09:18.350
observations using the new threshold,

00:09:18.360 --> 00:09:22.750
dosage less than 22.5.

00:09:22.760 --> 00:09:25.190
Now we calculate the similarity scores

00:09:25.200 --> 00:09:27.430
for the leaves

00:09:27.440 --> 00:09:34.870
and calculate the gain.

00:09:34.880 --> 00:09:38.510
The The for dosage less than 22.5

00:09:38.520 --> 00:09:40.830
is four.

00:09:40.840 --> 00:09:44.750
Since the gain for dosage less than 22.5

00:09:44.760 --> 00:09:47.030
is less than the gain for dosage less

00:09:47.040 --> 00:09:48.670
than 15,

00:09:48.680 --> 00:09:51.230
dosage less than 15 is better at

00:09:51.240 --> 00:09:53.510
splitting the residuals into clusters of

00:09:53.520 --> 00:09:56.510
similar values.

00:09:56.520 --> 00:09:59.110
Now we shift the threshold over so that

00:09:59.120 --> 00:10:01.030
it is the average of the last two

00:10:01.040 --> 00:10:02.950
observations

00:10:02.960 --> 00:10:05.230
and build a simple tree that divides the

00:10:05.240 --> 00:10:07.990
observations using the new threshold,

00:10:08.000 --> 00:10:10.990
dosage less than 30.

00:10:11.000 --> 00:10:13.430
Then we calculate the similarity scores

00:10:13.440 --> 00:10:15.430
for the leaves

00:10:15.440 --> 00:10:22.790
and the gain.

00:10:22.800 --> 00:10:25.870
The gain for dosage less than 30 equals

00:10:25.880 --> 00:10:29.150
56.33.

00:10:29.160 --> 00:10:31.910
Again, since the gain for dosage less

00:10:31.920 --> 00:10:34.750
than 30 is less than the gain for dosage

00:10:34.760 --> 00:10:36.710
less than 15,

00:10:36.720 --> 00:10:38.950
dosage less than 15 is better at

00:10:38.960 --> 00:10:41.870
splitting the observations.

00:10:41.880 --> 00:10:43.870
And since we can't shift the threshold

00:10:43.880 --> 00:10:46.110
over any further to the right, we are

00:10:46.120 --> 00:10:49.630
done comparing different thresholds.

00:10:49.640 --> 00:10:51.630
And we will use the threshold that gave

00:10:51.640 --> 00:10:54.190
us the largest gain, dosage less than

00:10:54.200 --> 00:10:58.710
15, for the first branch in the tree.

00:10:58.720 --> 00:11:01.750
Bam!

00:11:01.760 --> 00:11:04.630
Now, since there is only one residual in

00:11:04.640 --> 00:11:07.070
the leaf on the left, we can't split it

00:11:07.080 --> 00:11:08.829
any further.

00:11:08.839 --> 00:11:11.190
However, we can split the three

00:11:11.200 --> 00:11:14.350
residuals in the leaf on the right.

00:11:14.360 --> 00:11:17.990
So we start with these two observations

00:11:18.000 --> 00:11:21.230
and their average dosage is 22.5,

00:11:21.240 --> 00:11:23.510
which corresponds to this dotted green

00:11:23.520 --> 00:11:25.430
line.

00:11:25.440 --> 00:11:27.790
So the first threshold that we try is

00:11:27.800 --> 00:11:31.630
dosage less than 22.5.

00:11:31.640 --> 00:11:34.590
Now, just like before, we calculate the

00:11:34.600 --> 00:11:37.910
similarity scores for the leaves.

00:11:37.920 --> 00:11:40.790
Note, we calculated the similarity score

00:11:40.800 --> 00:11:42.830
for this node when we figured out how to

00:11:42.840 --> 00:11:45.550
split the root.

00:11:45.560 --> 00:11:53.110
So, now we calculate the gain.

00:11:53.120 --> 00:11:56.710
And we get gain equals 28.17

00:11:56.720 --> 00:11:58.950
for when the threshold is dosage less

00:11:58.960 --> 00:12:02.670
than 22.5.

00:12:02.680 --> 00:12:05.110
Now, we shift the threshold over so that

00:12:05.120 --> 00:12:07.190
it is the average of the last two

00:12:07.200 --> 00:12:09.150
observations,

00:12:09.160 --> 00:12:11.470
calculate the similarity scores for the

00:12:11.480 --> 00:12:12.990
leaves,

00:12:13.000 --> 00:12:17.310
and the gain.

00:12:17.320 --> 00:12:21.430
And we get gain equals 140.17,

00:12:21.440 --> 00:12:24.710
which is much larger than 28.17

00:12:24.720 --> 00:12:26.710
when the threshold was dosage less than

00:12:26.720 --> 00:12:29.910
22.5.

00:12:29.920 --> 00:12:32.590
So, we will use dosage less than 30 as

00:12:32.600 --> 00:12:36.070
the threshold for this branch.

00:12:36.080 --> 00:12:38.670
Note, to keep this example from getting

00:12:38.680 --> 00:12:41.550
out of hand, I've limited the tree depth

00:12:41.560 --> 00:12:43.750
to two levels.

00:12:43.760 --> 00:12:46.030
And this means we will not split this

00:12:46.040 --> 00:12:48.110
leaf any further, and we are done

00:12:48.120 --> 00:12:50.670
building this tree.

00:12:50.680 --> 00:12:53.670
However, the default is to allow up to

00:12:53.680 --> 00:12:55.670
six levels.

00:12:55.680 --> 00:12:58.270
Small bam.

00:12:58.280 --> 00:13:00.390
Now, we need to talk about how to prune

00:13:00.400 --> 00:13:02.430
this tree.

00:13:02.440 --> 00:13:05.430
We prune an XGBoost tree based on its

00:13:05.440 --> 00:13:07.790
gain values.

00:13:07.800 --> 00:13:10.150
We start by picking a number, for

00:13:10.160 --> 00:13:13.190
example, 130.

00:13:13.200 --> 00:13:15.750
Oh, no! It's the dreaded terminology

00:13:15.760 --> 00:13:17.070
alert.

00:13:17.080 --> 00:13:21.950
XGBoost calls this number gamma.

00:13:21.960 --> 00:13:24.110
We then calculate the difference between

00:13:24.120 --> 00:13:26.230
the gain associated with the lowest

00:13:26.240 --> 00:13:28.430
branch in the tree

00:13:28.440 --> 00:13:31.310
and the value for gamma.

00:13:31.320 --> 00:13:33.670
If the difference between the gain and

00:13:33.680 --> 00:13:36.430
gamma is negative, we will remove the

00:13:36.440 --> 00:13:38.110
branch.

00:13:38.120 --> 00:13:40.150
And if the difference between the gain

00:13:40.160 --> 00:13:42.910
and gamma is positive, we will not

00:13:42.920 --> 00:13:45.310
remove the branch.

00:13:45.320 --> 00:13:48.870
In this case, when we plug in the gain

00:13:48.880 --> 00:13:52.990
and the value for gamma, 130,

00:13:53.000 --> 00:13:55.430
we get a positive number. So, we will

00:13:55.440 --> 00:13:57.830
not remove this branch and we are done

00:13:57.840 --> 00:14:00.430
pruning.

00:14:00.440 --> 00:14:04.710
Note, the gain for the root, 120.3,

00:14:04.720 --> 00:14:08.270
is less than 130, the value for gamma,

00:14:08.280 --> 00:14:11.270
so the difference will be negative.

00:14:11.280 --> 00:14:13.710
However, because we did not remove the

00:14:13.720 --> 00:14:16.270
first branch, we will not remove the

00:14:16.280 --> 00:14:18.350
root.

00:14:18.360 --> 00:14:21.230
In contrast, if we set gamma equal to

00:14:21.240 --> 00:14:24.270
150, then we would remove this branch

00:14:24.280 --> 00:14:25.950
because

00:14:25.960 --> 00:14:28.470
140.17

00:14:28.480 --> 00:14:30.430
- 150

00:14:30.440 --> 00:14:33.430
equals a negative number.

00:14:33.440 --> 00:14:36.950
So, let's remove this branch.

00:14:36.960 --> 00:14:39.590
Now, we subtract gamma from the gain for

00:14:39.600 --> 00:14:41.550
the root.

00:14:41.560 --> 00:14:44.470
Since 120.33

00:14:44.480 --> 00:14:49.030
- 150 equals a negative number,

00:14:49.040 --> 00:14:51.990
we will remove the root.

00:14:52.000 --> 00:14:54.030
And all we would be left with is the

00:14:54.040 --> 00:14:56.190
original prediction, which is pretty

00:14:56.200 --> 00:14:59.070
extreme pruning.

00:14:59.080 --> 00:15:01.990
So, while this wasn't the most nuanced

00:15:02.000 --> 00:15:04.510
example of how an XGBoost tree is

00:15:04.520 --> 00:15:08.550
pruned, I hope you get the idea.

00:15:08.560 --> 00:15:10.390
Now, let's go back to the original

00:15:10.400 --> 00:15:12.110
residuals

00:15:12.120 --> 00:15:15.590
and build a tree just like before,

00:15:15.600 --> 00:15:18.030
only this time, when we calculate

00:15:18.040 --> 00:15:21.110
similarity scores, we will set lambda

00:15:21.120 --> 00:15:23.790
equal to 1.

00:15:23.800 --> 00:15:26.630
Remember, lambda is a regularization

00:15:26.640 --> 00:15:28.630
parameter, which means that it is

00:15:28.640 --> 00:15:30.630
intended to reduce the prediction

00:15:30.640 --> 00:15:34.910
sensitivity to individual observations.

00:15:34.920 --> 00:15:37.430
Now, the similarity score for the root

00:15:37.440 --> 00:15:38.910
is

00:15:38.920 --> 00:15:40.750
3.2,

00:15:40.760 --> 00:15:43.830
which is 8/10 of what we got when lambda

00:15:43.840 --> 00:15:46.270
equals 0.

00:15:46.280 --> 00:15:48.470
When we calculate the similarity score

00:15:48.480 --> 00:15:50.910
for the leaf on the left,

00:15:50.920 --> 00:15:53.670
we get 55.12,

00:15:53.680 --> 00:15:55.790
which is half of what we got when lambda

00:15:55.800 --> 00:15:58.310
equals 0.

00:15:58.320 --> 00:16:00.230
And when we calculate the similarity

00:16:00.240 --> 00:16:02.910
score for the leaf on the right,

00:16:02.920 --> 00:16:05.150
we get 10.56,

00:16:05.160 --> 00:16:07.870
which is 3/4 of what we got when lambda

00:16:07.880 --> 00:16:10.390
equals 0.

00:16:10.400 --> 00:16:13.430
So, one thing we see is that when lambda

00:16:13.440 --> 00:16:16.470
is greater than 0, the similarity scores

00:16:16.480 --> 00:16:18.230
are smaller.

00:16:18.240 --> 00:16:20.830
And the amount of decrease is inversely

00:16:20.840 --> 00:16:23.270
proportional to the number of residuals

00:16:23.280 --> 00:16:25.430
in the node.

00:16:25.440 --> 00:16:28.110
In other words, the leaf on the left had

00:16:28.120 --> 00:16:30.310
only one residual, and it had the

00:16:30.320 --> 00:16:32.990
largest decrease in similarity score,

00:16:33.000 --> 00:16:35.310
50%.

00:16:35.320 --> 00:16:38.030
In contrast, the root had all four

00:16:38.040 --> 00:16:40.950
residuals and the smallest decrease,

00:16:40.960 --> 00:16:43.510
20%.

00:16:43.520 --> 00:16:46.790
Now, when we calculate the gain,

00:16:46.800 --> 00:16:49.710
we get 66, which is a lot less than

00:16:49.720 --> 00:16:52.030
120.33,

00:16:52.040 --> 00:16:56.230
the value we got when lambda equals 0.

00:16:56.240 --> 00:16:59.710
Similarly, when lambda equals 1, the

00:16:59.720 --> 00:17:02.190
gain for the next branch is smaller than

00:17:02.200 --> 00:17:04.390
before.

00:17:04.400 --> 00:17:07.630
Now, just for comparison, these were the

00:17:07.640 --> 00:17:11.550
gain values when lambda equals 0.

00:17:11.560 --> 00:17:13.270
When we first talked about pruning

00:17:13.280 --> 00:17:17.870
trees, we set gamma equal to 130.

00:17:17.880 --> 00:17:20.510
And because for the lowest branch in the

00:17:20.520 --> 00:17:23.870
first tree, gain minus gamma equal to

00:17:23.880 --> 00:17:26.470
positive number, so we did not prune it

00:17:26.480 --> 00:17:28.510
all.

00:17:28.520 --> 00:17:32.070
Now, with lambda equals 1, the values

00:17:32.080 --> 00:17:35.950
for gain are both less than 130.

00:17:35.960 --> 00:17:40.510
So, we would prune the whole tree away.

00:17:40.520 --> 00:17:43.750
So, when lambda is greater than zero, it

00:17:43.760 --> 00:17:45.750
is easier to prune leaves because the

00:17:45.760 --> 00:17:49.670
values for gain are smaller.

00:17:49.680 --> 00:17:52.510
Note, before we move on, I want to

00:17:52.520 --> 00:17:56.550
illustrate one last feature of lambda.

00:17:56.560 --> 00:17:59.110
For this example, imagine we split this

00:17:59.120 --> 00:18:02.190
node into two leaves.

00:18:02.200 --> 00:18:04.150
Now, let's calculate the similarity

00:18:04.160 --> 00:18:08.350
scores with lambda equal to 1.

00:18:08.360 --> 00:18:10.790
For the branch, we get

00:18:10.800 --> 00:18:13.910
65.3.

00:18:13.920 --> 00:18:19.710
For the left leaf, we get 21.12.

00:18:19.720 --> 00:18:22.310
And for the right leaf, we get

00:18:22.320 --> 00:18:25.830
28.12.

00:18:25.840 --> 00:18:29.150
That means the gain is

00:18:29.160 --> 00:18:33.430
-16.06.

00:18:33.440 --> 00:18:35.950
Now, when we decide if we should prune

00:18:35.960 --> 00:18:39.030
this branch, we plug in the gain

00:18:39.040 --> 00:18:41.870
and we plug in a value for gamma.

00:18:41.880 --> 00:18:45.870
Note, if we set gamma equal to zero,

00:18:45.880 --> 00:18:49.150
then we will get a negative number.

00:18:49.160 --> 00:18:51.510
And we will prune this branch even

00:18:51.520 --> 00:18:54.750
though gamma equals zero.

00:18:54.760 --> 00:18:57.350
In other words, setting gamma equal to

00:18:57.360 --> 00:19:01.230
zero does not turn off pruning.

00:19:01.240 --> 00:19:04.510
Dang.

00:19:04.520 --> 00:19:07.190
On the other hand, by setting lambda

00:19:07.200 --> 00:19:09.790
equal to 1, lambda did what it was

00:19:09.800 --> 00:19:12.670
supposed to do. It prevented overfitting

00:19:12.680 --> 00:19:14.630
the training data.

00:19:14.640 --> 00:19:17.230
Awesome.

00:19:17.240 --> 00:19:20.830
For now, regardless of lambda and gamma,

00:19:20.840 --> 00:19:22.910
let's assume that this is the tree we

00:19:22.920 --> 00:19:24.710
are working with

00:19:24.720 --> 00:19:26.990
and determine the output values for the

00:19:27.000 --> 00:19:29.030
leaves.

00:19:29.040 --> 00:19:32.550
The output value equals the sum of the

00:19:32.560 --> 00:19:35.830
residuals divided by the number of

00:19:35.840 --> 00:19:39.110
residuals plus lambda.

00:19:39.120 --> 00:19:42.470
Note, the output value equation is like

00:19:42.480 --> 00:19:45.230
the similarity score, except we do not

00:19:45.240 --> 00:19:48.510
square the sum of the residuals.

00:19:48.520 --> 00:19:51.070
So, for this leaf,

00:19:51.080 --> 00:19:55.550
we plug in the residual, -10.5,

00:19:55.560 --> 00:19:57.910
the number of residuals in the leaf,

00:19:57.920 --> 00:19:59.790
one,

00:19:59.800 --> 00:20:01.790
and the value for the regularization

00:20:01.800 --> 00:20:04.950
parameter, lambda.

00:20:04.960 --> 00:20:07.630
If lambda equals zero, then there is no

00:20:07.640 --> 00:20:10.350
regularization, and the output value

00:20:10.360 --> 00:20:14.070
equals -10.5.

00:20:14.080 --> 00:20:17.870
On the other hand, if lambda equals one,

00:20:17.880 --> 00:20:23.030
the output value equals -5.25.

00:20:23.040 --> 00:20:25.310
In other words, when lambda is greater

00:20:25.320 --> 00:20:27.590
than zero, then it will reduce the

00:20:27.600 --> 00:20:29.870
amount that this individual observation

00:20:29.880 --> 00:20:33.190
adds to the overall prediction.

00:20:33.200 --> 00:20:36.070
Thus, lambda, the regularization

00:20:36.080 --> 00:20:38.390
parameter, will reduce the prediction

00:20:38.400 --> 00:20:40.510
sensitivity to this individual

00:20:40.520 --> 00:20:42.790
observation.

00:20:42.800 --> 00:20:45.350
For now, we'll keep things simple and

00:20:45.360 --> 00:20:48.230
let lambda equal zero, because this is

00:20:48.240 --> 00:20:50.270
the default value,

00:20:50.280 --> 00:20:52.270
and put -10.5

00:20:52.280 --> 00:20:56.150
under the leaf, so we will remember it.

00:20:56.160 --> 00:20:58.230
Now, let's calculate the output value

00:20:58.240 --> 00:21:00.630
for this leaf.

00:21:00.640 --> 00:21:03.230
When lambda equals zero, the output

00:21:03.240 --> 00:21:04.910
value is

00:21:04.920 --> 00:21:06.750
seven.

00:21:06.760 --> 00:21:09.990
In other words, when lambda equals zero,

00:21:10.000 --> 00:21:12.590
the output value for a leaf is simply

00:21:12.600 --> 00:21:14.990
the average of the residuals in that

00:21:15.000 --> 00:21:17.070
leaf.

00:21:17.080 --> 00:21:19.310
So, we'll put the output value under the

00:21:19.320 --> 00:21:22.830
leaf so we will remember it.

00:21:22.840 --> 00:21:26.030
Lastly, when lambda equals zero, the

00:21:26.040 --> 00:21:28.990
output value for this leaf is

00:21:29.000 --> 00:21:32.750
-7.5.

00:21:32.760 --> 00:21:35.750
Now, at long last, the first tree is

00:21:35.760 --> 00:21:37.150
complete.

00:21:37.160 --> 00:21:40.230
Double bam!

00:21:40.240 --> 00:21:42.670
Since we have built our first tree, we

00:21:42.680 --> 00:21:45.390
can make new predictions.

00:21:45.400 --> 00:21:48.710
And just like on extreme gradient boost,

00:21:48.720 --> 00:21:51.390
XG boost makes new predictions by

00:21:51.400 --> 00:21:54.110
starting with the initial prediction

00:21:54.120 --> 00:21:56.830
and adding the output of the tree scaled

00:21:56.840 --> 00:21:58.950
by a learning rate.

00:21:58.960 --> 00:22:01.750
Oh, no! It's another dreaded terminology

00:22:01.760 --> 00:22:03.750
alert.

00:22:03.760 --> 00:22:07.470
XG boost calls the learning rate eta,

00:22:07.480 --> 00:22:10.630
and the default value is 0.3.

00:22:10.640 --> 00:22:13.070
So, that's what we'll use.

00:22:13.080 --> 00:22:15.630
Thus, the new predicted value for this

00:22:15.640 --> 00:22:19.430
observation with dosage equal to 10

00:22:19.440 --> 00:22:23.510
is the original prediction 0.5

00:22:23.520 --> 00:22:28.270
plus the learning rate eta 0.3

00:22:28.280 --> 00:22:32.750
times the output value -10.5.

00:22:32.760 --> 00:22:37.350
And that gives us -2.65.

00:22:37.360 --> 00:22:41.950
So, if the original prediction was 0.5,

00:22:41.960 --> 00:22:45.790
then this was the original residual.

00:22:45.800 --> 00:22:49.910
The new prediction is -2.65.

00:22:49.920 --> 00:22:52.070
And we see that the new residual is

00:22:52.080 --> 00:22:54.390
smaller than before. So, we've taken a

00:22:54.400 --> 00:22:57.630
small step in the right direction.

00:22:57.640 --> 00:23:00.470
Similarly, the new prediction for this

00:23:00.480 --> 00:23:04.430
observation with dosage equal 20, is

00:23:04.440 --> 00:23:06.950
beep beep.

00:23:06.960 --> 00:23:09.190
2.6.

00:23:09.200 --> 00:23:11.190
And the new residual is smaller than

00:23:11.200 --> 00:23:13.630
before. So, we've taken another small

00:23:13.640 --> 00:23:16.950
step in the right direction.

00:23:16.960 --> 00:23:19.350
Likewise, the new predictions for the

00:23:19.360 --> 00:23:21.390
remaining observations have smaller

00:23:21.400 --> 00:23:24.030
residuals than before, suggesting each

00:23:24.040 --> 00:23:27.510
small step was in the right direction.

00:23:27.520 --> 00:23:30.190
Bam!

00:23:30.200 --> 00:23:32.310
Now, we build another tree based on the

00:23:32.320 --> 00:23:34.150
new residuals

00:23:34.160 --> 00:23:36.030
and make new predictions that give us

00:23:36.040 --> 00:23:39.070
even smaller residuals.

00:23:39.080 --> 00:23:41.430
And then build another tree based on the

00:23:41.440 --> 00:23:43.990
newest residuals.

00:23:44.000 --> 00:23:45.910
And we keep building trees until the

00:23:45.920 --> 00:23:48.550
residuals are super small or we have

00:23:48.560 --> 00:23:51.790
reached the maximum number.

00:23:51.800 --> 00:23:54.790
Triple bam!

00:23:54.800 --> 00:23:57.950
In summary, when building XGBoost trees

00:23:57.960 --> 00:24:00.270
for regression,

00:24:00.280 --> 00:24:04.390
we calculate similarity scores

00:24:04.400 --> 00:24:06.910
and gain to determine how to split the

00:24:06.920 --> 00:24:09.550
data.

00:24:09.560 --> 00:24:11.950
And we prune the tree by calculating the

00:24:11.960 --> 00:24:14.550
differences between gain values and a

00:24:14.560 --> 00:24:17.550
user-defined tree complexity parameter,

00:24:17.560 --> 00:24:19.710
gamma.

00:24:19.720 --> 00:24:21.910
If the difference is positive, then we

00:24:21.920 --> 00:24:23.590
do not prune.

00:24:23.600 --> 00:24:27.590
If it's negative, then we prune.

00:24:27.600 --> 00:24:30.670
For example, if we subtract gamma from

00:24:30.680 --> 00:24:33.670
this gain and get a negative value, we

00:24:33.680 --> 00:24:37.470
will prune. Otherwise, we're done.

00:24:37.480 --> 00:24:40.230
If we prune, then we will subtract gamma

00:24:40.240 --> 00:24:42.550
from the next gain value and work our

00:24:42.560 --> 00:24:44.870
way up the tree.

00:24:44.880 --> 00:24:47.150
Then, we calculate the output values for

00:24:47.160 --> 00:24:49.670
the remaining leaves.

00:24:49.680 --> 00:24:52.430
And lastly, lambda is a regularization

00:24:52.440 --> 00:24:54.830
parameter, and when lambda is greater

00:24:54.840 --> 00:24:57.910
than zero, it results in more pruning by

00:24:57.920 --> 00:25:00.270
shrinking the similarity scores.

00:25:00.280 --> 00:25:02.510
And it results in smaller output values

00:25:02.520 --> 00:25:04.230
for the leaves.

00:25:04.240 --> 00:25:06.150
Bam.

00:25:06.160 --> 00:25:09.510
Tune in next time for XGBoost part two,

00:25:09.520 --> 00:25:12.310
when we give an overview of how XGBoost

00:25:12.320 --> 00:25:15.030
trees are built for classification.

00:25:15.040 --> 00:25:18.190
It's going to be totally awesome.

00:25:18.200 --> 00:25:20.870
Hooray! We've made it to the end of

00:25:20.880 --> 00:25:23.670
another exciting StatQuest. If you like

00:25:23.680 --> 00:25:25.870
this StatQuest and want to see more,

00:25:25.880 --> 00:25:27.870
please subscribe. And if you want to

